The best and most professional approach is **Option 2: Trigger the lock when the user clicks the "Next" button.**

**Do not trigger the lock automatically when they click a row in the table.**
Here is why: Users frequently click around tables to read descriptions or examine different VIDs before making a final decision. If you trigger the `/lock` API on every row click, you will spam the backend with lock/unlock requests, and if a user clicks row A, then row B, row A might get stuck in a locked state momentarily.

By tying the lock to the **Next** button, you confirm their intent. The exact microsecond they attempt to proceed, you lock the VID. If they click "Previous" to go back, you unlock it.

Here is the exact code to seamlessly integrate this microsecond lock/unlock lifecycle into your **`VoucherPosting.jsx`** component.

### 1. Track the Locked VID

At the top of your `VoucherPosting` component, add a `useRef` to track which VID is currently locked. We use a `ref` instead of state so cleanup functions (like unmounting) can instantly access the current value.

```javascript
  // --- ADD THIS WITH YOUR OTHER REFS ---
  const lockedVidRef = useRef(null);

```

### 2. Update the `handleNext` Function (The Lock Acquisition)

Replace your existing `handleNext` function with this asynchronous version. It intercepts the user between Step 0 and Step 1, acquires the lock, and blocks them if User A beat them to it.

```javascript
  const handleNext = async () => {
    if (activeStep === 0) {
      if (!selectedVoucher) {
        showSnackBar("Please select a voucher from the list first.", "warning");
        return;
      }

      // --- ACQUIRE THE PESSIMISTIC LOCK ---
      const vidToLock = selectedVoucher.vid || selectedVoucher.VID;
      setIsPageLoading(true);
      
      try {
        await callApi(`/JS/voucher/lock/${vidToLock}`, null, "POST");
        lockedVidRef.current = vidToLock; // Store the successfully locked VID
        setActiveStep((prevStep) => prevStep + 1); // Proceed to Entry Method
      } catch (error) {
        // If 409 Conflict is returned, User A already has it.
        const errMsg = error?.response?.data?.message || "This VID is already in use by another user.";
        showSnackBar(errMsg, "warning");
        
        // Optional: Call fetchInitialData() here to refresh the grid and remove the taken VID
      } finally {
        setIsPageLoading(false);
      }
    } else {
      // Normal progression for other steps
      setActiveStep((prevStep) => prevStep + 1);
    }
  };

```

### 3. Build the Microsecond Release Function

Add this helper function below `handleNext`. It instantly drops the lock.

```javascript
  const releaseLock = useCallback(async (vid) => {
    if (vid) {
      try {
        await callApi(`/JS/voucher/unlock/${vid}`, null, "POST");
        lockedVidRef.current = null; // Clear the ref
      } catch (e) {
        console.error("Failed to release VID lock", e);
      }
    }
  }, [callApi]);

```

